<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Igodomigodo (Ogiso dynasty)</h1><p class="st-meta">900 CE – 1180 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Edo kingdom in what is now southern Nigeria, ruled by the Ogisos or 'kings of the sky' before the Oba dynasty of Benin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Known almost entirely from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-people">Edo</a> oral tradition and archaeology. According to tradition, the Edo villages of the forest west of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> were united under a line of rulers called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogiso-dynasty">Ogiso</a>, 'ruler of the sky', the first of whom was Igodo. The kingdom took its name, Igodomigodo, from him, and is traditionally dated from around the 10th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tradition counts some 31 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogiso-dynasty">Ogisos</a>, who ruled with chiefs drawn from the leading lineages, the forerunners of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzama">Uzama</a> nobility. Craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> in wood and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and the first of the earthen walls around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> are attributed to this era. Kingship was sacred and tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-people">Edo</a> religion, ancestor worship and the cult of the land.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogiso-dynasty">Ogiso</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owodo">Owodo</a>, was banished, and after an interregnum under the administrators Evian and Ogiamien the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-people">Edo</a> chiefs invited Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oranmiyan">Oranmiyan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ife">Kingdom of Ife</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eweka-i">Eweka I</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba dynasty</a> around 1180, and Igodomigodo became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>, which kept many Ogiso-era titles and rituals.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 900 CE – 1180 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:77.61%;width:5.57%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 CE – 1180 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Igodo, Ere, Owodo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Edo traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Edo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Igodomigodo, Ogiso kingdom, Kingdom of the Ogisos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1040&amp;focus=igodomigodo-ogiso-dynasty" aria-label="Igodomigodo (Ogiso dynasty) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4888 1817 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Igodomigodo (Ogiso dynasty) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5155 2197h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5208 2073h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5208 2073h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Nigeria. <a href="/atlas?year=1040&amp;focus=igodomigodo-ogiso-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
